
aifoundry1 - AI Foundry lab machine with 2 ET-SoC-1 cards (driver et_soc1 0.20.0)
  card 0  PCI 0000:01:00.0  /dev/et0_mgmt /dev/et0_ops  firmware 1.4.1
  card 1  PCI 0000:02:00.0  /dev/et1_mgmt /dev/et1_ops  firmware 1.2.0
  Clocks as of 2026-09-27: card 1 read 600 MHz in every sample since 25 Sep, cool or hot (its DVFS appears to be
  off); card 0 idles at 300 MHz and its governor acts only while a kernel runs.

  WARNING, card 0 OVERHEATS under load: on 2026-09-25 about ten minutes of short test launches took its die to
  115-117 C (card 1 stayed near 70 C), and the driver counted 18 board-power (75 W) and 10 thermal-throttle
  events (cat /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/err_stats/ce_count). Do not run work on card 0 until its fan,
  heat sink and airflow have been checked.

* Each device node can be open in ONE process at a time. Who is using a card: et-who (in scripts: et-who --check)
* Reserve card N for a run:  flock /run/lock/etsoc-shire<N>.lock <command>
* /opt/et/bin/dev_mngt_service and et-powertop open BOTH cards' management nodes, even with -n <N>: run them only
  when both cards are free. Programs built here against /opt/et honour ET_DEVICES=<n> (open only card n).
* These commands change a card for every user: reset, trace level, telemetry stats reset (ettelem --reset-ms),
  temperature thresholds, TDP, frequency, active power management.
* Stop tools and samplers with Ctrl-C or plain kill, never kill -9: a request cut off mid-way leaves a
  stale reply, and the next program that opens the card dies with std::bad_function_call. To clear it once:
  /opt/et/bin/dev_mngt_service -m DM_CMD_GET_MODULE_POWER -n <N> -u 5000
* Vendor tools: /opt/et/bin (on PATH in login shells). Link /opt/et/lib with -Wl,-rpath,/opt/et/lib.
  This machine's /opt/et is a May 2026 rebuild (et-platform fork acc7ed25). aifoundry2 runs the stock
  et-platform 353f20e build.
* Host programs: register the runtime's log levels first in main (a race in libetrt crashes about one launch
  in 100 at 1.08 s otherwise): see registerRuntimeLogLevels() in github.com/yaroslavvb/et-soc1-prototyping.
* A GitHub Actions runner (nekkoai/hf-hackathon) runs on this machine and can use the cards.
* The disk is nearly full (ZFS pool 95%). Keep one copy of each large model; delete what you do not use.
* /tmp is cleared at every boot: keep your work in your home directory.
* Crash dumps of your programs: coredumpctl list ; coredumpctl gdb <pid>
* Machine facts to record with a measurement: et-lab-manifest.  Health check (read-only): et-lab-health
* Lab accounts and notes: https://spacesheep.dev/@yaroslavvb/aifoundry-lab-accounts
